 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Replacement
+
+Use two queues whose combined cached JPEG data shares the 12 GiB budget:
+
+- **A1in** holds sheets after their first completed cache admission, ordered by admission time. It uses FIFO replacement and has a configurable byte threshold, `Kin`, initially set to 25% of the cache budget (3 GiB).
+- **Am** holds sheets that received a cache hit while in A1in, ordered from least to most recently used.
+
+A cache hit in A1in serves the sheet and promotes it to the most-recently-used end of Am. A cache hit in Am serves the sheet and refreshes its recency. Requests that only share a pending render do not count as cache hits or cause promotion. Promoting a sheet changes only its queue membership and ordering; it does not change the total cached bytes.
+
+When admission requires space, evict the oldest A1in entry while A1in occupies more than `Kin`; otherwise evict the least-recently-used Am entry. Repeat this choice until the new sheet fits. If the queues contain no evictable sheet sufficient to admit the completed render, return the rendered JPEG without caching it. A sheet evicted from A1in leaves no replacement history, so a later admission starts again as first-use. This keeps replacement to two queues and avoids retaining identifiers for sheets no longer cached.
+
+`Kin` is a replacement threshold, not a separate disk reservation: A1in and Am consume one shared budget, and unused capacity in either queue remains available to the other. The byte-based threshold accommodates sheets whose sizes differ from the typical 2 MiB. Queue membership, ordering, and byte accounting remain private to the preview worker.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|-------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q with FIFO A1in and LRU Am | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Protects repeatedly used comparison sheets from one-off screening scans without maintaining popularity counters or aging them across project switches. The additional queue bookkeeping is contained within the preview worker. |
+| Replacement history | Forget an A1in key on eviction | Retain evicted keys in a separate A1out ghost queue | Preserves the two-queue design and its bounded metadata; a retained-key history would add another queue and another sizing decision. |
+| A1in sizing unit | Cached JPEG bytes, initially 3 GiB | A fixed number of entries | A byte threshold shares the existing disk budget predictably even when contact-sheet sizes vary. The initial split is tunable from observed screening and comparison workloads. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
